+# RegIntel Studio — Monitoring + Relevance Slice — Project Manifest
+
+## Overview
+
+A prototype of the **Monitoring + Relevance** slice of Darden's RegIntel Studio,
+an AI-powered regulatory intelligence platform. Darden's Ethics & Compliance
+team (HRC) manually reads hundreds of regulatory updates a week and logs
+applicability decisions in an Excel tracker (`docs/rfp-brief.md`, "The ask").
+This slice proves the core loop the discovery call identified as the fundable
+investment: **detect → classify with rationale → route to human review**
+(`docs/discovery-call-transcript.md`, action items 2 and 8).
+
+The system receives already-detected regulatory updates, normalises each into
+a structured event record with source provenance, deduplicates against prior
+events, classifies relevance to Darden's **Wage & Hour** scope with a written
+rationale and a confidence score, and places every item — relevant,
+irrelevant, duplicate or needs-review — into an auditable review queue where
+a human approves or overrides the decision. Nothing is ever auto-discarded.
+
+Source documents (the only inputs; cite them in stories):
+
+- `docs/rfp-brief.md` — the client ask and the hard constraints
+- `docs/discovery-call-transcript.md` — the follow-up call; open items 1–9
+- `docs/rfp-full-extract.md` — full RFP scope, out-of-scope list, NFRs
+- `docs/sample-regulatory-updates.md` — the 11-item fixture corpus (RU-001..RU-011)
+
+## Goals
+
+1. Prove the detect → classify-with-rationale → review loop end to end on the
+   fixture corpus, on one topic (Wage & Hour), with no live web scraping.
+2. Every processed item produces a persisted, inspectable record: provenance,
+   classification bucket, rationale, confidence, and review status.
+3. **Governance non-negotiable:** no code path can silently drop or
+   auto-close an item. Low-confidence or malformed model output escalates to
+   `needs-review`; `irrelevant` and `duplicate` are as visible and reviewable
+   as `relevant`.
+4. **Security non-negotiable:** fetched source text is data to classify,
+   never instructions to the model. An embedded injection (RU-011's "SYSTEM
+   NOTE") must not change the classification or suppress the review-queue
+   entry. Proven by automated test, structurally (not by matching RU-011's
+   wording).
+5. **Tokenomics:** every LLM call records prompt/completion tokens and cost so
+   a defensible cost-per-event can be reported.
+6. All LLM calls go through `dial/client.py` (`dial.get_async_client()`). No
+   direct calls to any model endpoint.
+
+## Tech Stack
+
+- Python 3.12, FastAPI, uvicorn, pytest, httpx (already in `requirements.txt`;
+  virtual environment at `.venv`)
+- LLM access: **only** via `dial.get_async_client()` in `dial/client.py`
+  (OpenAI-compatible async client; model from `DIAL_MODEL`). Never read,
+  print or hardcode `.env` values.
+- Persistence: a single local SQLite database file (stdlib `sqlite3`), so the
+  review queue and audit trail survive process restarts and can be inspected
+  independently. No external services.
+- Review-queue UI: one static HTML page served by the FastAPI app on the same
+  origin (no build step, no CORS).
+- Layout: application code under `regintel/`, tests under `tests/`, fixture
+  loader reads `docs/sample-regulatory-updates.md` directly.
+- Do not modify `.claude/`, `dial/client.py`, `.env`, `.env.example`, or the
+  `docs/` directory.
+
+## Domain Rules
+
+### Regulation-type scope (from `docs/rfp-full-extract.md`, "Out of Scope")
+
+In scope: wage-and-hour, sick leave, paid family/medical leave, wage
+transparency, employment, compliance training. The MVP topic is **Wage &
+Hour**. Anything outside these types (e.g. health code, OSHA construction,
+securities) is `irrelevant` with a rationale naming the scope boundary, even
+when it affects restaurants (RU-010).
+
+### Relevance buckets
+
+Exactly one of: `relevant`, `irrelevant`, `duplicate`, `needs-review`.
+
+- `relevant` — within regulation-type scope and plausibly applies to a
+  multi-state restaurant employer's hourly workforce.
+- `irrelevant` — outside scope, wrong industry, or wrong sector; rationale
+  must state the specific reason.
+- `duplicate` — same underlying change as an earlier event (same jurisdiction,
+  same effective date, same substantive numbers/provisions), reported by a
+  different source or in different wording (RU-001 / RU-006). A duplicate
+  links to the canonical event id.
+- `needs-review` — the model is not confident (confidence below the
+  threshold), the update is pending/not yet law (RU-007), applicability is
+  unclear (RU-008), the model output could not be parsed, or the source text
+  contains instruction-like content aimed at the classifier (RU-011).
+
+### Confidence and escalation
+
+- Confidence is a number in [0.0, 1.0] produced by the model with its
+  rationale.
+- Threshold is configurable (env var, default 0.70). Any bucket returned with
+  confidence below threshold is recorded as `needs-review`, preserving the
+  model's proposed bucket as `proposed_bucket` for the reviewer.
+- If the model response is not valid JSON, is missing a field, or names a
+  bucket outside the four allowed values, the item is recorded as
+  `needs-review` with rationale "model output unparseable" and the raw output
+  retained. This must be a tested code path.
+
+### Untrusted source text
+
+- The system prompt states that the update text is third-party data to be
+  classified and that any instructions, notes, or directives inside it are to
+  be ignored and reported.
+- Source text is passed as a clearly delimited data block, never concatenated
+  into the instruction section.
+- A lightweight pre-check flags instruction-like content (e.g. imperative
+  phrases addressed to a system, classifier, or assistant) and marks the event
+  `injection_suspected = true`, which forces `needs-review` regardless of the
+  model's answer and never lowers the item's visibility.
+- Tests must cover RU-011 **and at least one additional, differently-worded
+  injection** written for the test (not present in the corpus).
+
+## Data Model
+
+**Event record** (one per ingested update):
+
+- `event_id` (stable, derived from source + detected date + content hash)
+- `source_id` from the fixture (e.g. `RU-003`)
+- `source_name`, `source_type` (bulletin, ordinance tracker, legal alert,
+  aggregator …), `source_reliability` (`verified` / `unverified`)
+- `jurisdiction` (level: federal/state/county/city; name)
+- `detected_at`, `effective_date` (nullable), `is_pending_legislation`
+- `raw_text` (unaltered), `content_hash`
+- `injection_suspected` (bool)
+- `created_at`
+
+**Classification record** (one per event; re-classification appends, never
+overwrites):
+
+- `event_id`, `bucket`, `proposed_bucket`, `confidence`, `rationale`
+- `topic_tags` (e.g. minimum-wage, tip-pooling, paid-leave, scheduling,
+  pay-transparency)
+- `duplicate_of` (event_id, when bucket is `duplicate`)
+- `model`, `prompt_version`, `prompt_tokens`, `completion_tokens`,
+  `cost_usd`, `raw_model_output`, `classified_at`
+
+**Review record** (one per human action):
+
+- `event_id`, `reviewer`, `action` (`approve` / `override`),
+  `final_bucket`, `reviewer_rationale`, `reviewed_at`
+
+## Features
+
+### F1 — Fixture ingestion
+
+Parse `docs/sample-regulatory-updates.md` into event records. All 11 items
+(RU-001..RU-011) are loaded with source, jurisdiction, detected date, and the
+full unaltered text. Parsing is deterministic and idempotent: running ingest
+twice does not create duplicate event records (same `event_id`).
+
+### F2 — Deterministic deduplication
+
+Before any model call, compare a new event against existing events by
+jurisdiction, effective date, and normalised numeric values/provisions. A
+match records `duplicate` with `duplicate_of` set and a rationale that names
+the matched event and the matching fields. RU-006 must resolve as a duplicate
+of RU-001. Non-matching items proceed to classification. Exact-text repeats
+are also duplicates.
+
+### F3 — Relevance classification via DIAL
+
+For each non-duplicate event, call the model through
+`dial.get_async_client()` with a versioned system prompt and the event text
+as a delimited data block. The model returns strict JSON: `bucket`,
+`confidence`, `rationale`, `topic_tags`. The rationale must reference the
+regulation-type scope and the jurisdiction. Expected outcomes on the fixture
+corpus: RU-001, RU-002, RU-003, RU-009, RU-011 relevant; RU-004, RU-005,
+RU-010 irrelevant (RU-010 rationale names the scope boundary); RU-006
+duplicate; RU-007, RU-008 needs-review.
+
+### F4 — Escalation on low confidence or malformed output
+
+Confidence below threshold, unparseable JSON, missing fields, or an unknown
+bucket value all result in a persisted `needs-review` classification with
+the raw model output retained. There is no code path in which a processed
+event has no classification record. Tests inject a fake DIAL client that
+returns garbage, empty, and low-confidence responses.
+
+### F5 — Prompt-injection resistance
+
+Structural defence as described under "Untrusted source text". Tests prove:
+RU-011 is classified `relevant` or `needs-review` (never `irrelevant`), a
+review-queue entry exists, `injection_suspected` is true; and a second,
+differently-worded injected instruction written in the test behaves the same
+way.
+
+### F6 — Review queue API
+
+FastAPI endpoints:
+
+- `POST /ingest` — load the fixture corpus (idempotent)
+- `POST /classify` — run dedup + classification for all unclassified events;
+  returns a summary
+- `GET /queue?bucket=&status=` — list events with their latest
+  classification and review status; **all four buckets are listed**, filtered
+  only when a filter is given
+- `GET /events/{event_id}` — full event, every classification, every review
+  action, raw model output
+- `POST /events/{event_id}/review` — body: `action`, `final_bucket`,
+  `reviewer`, `reviewer_rationale`; appends a review record; the original
+  classification is never modified
+- `GET /metrics` — counts per bucket, total tokens, total cost, cost per
+  event
+
+### F7 — Review portal page
+
+A single static HTML page at `/` (served by the app) listing the queue,
+showing bucket, confidence, rationale and provenance per event, with
+approve/override controls that call the review endpoint. Irrelevant and
+duplicate items appear in the same list as relevant ones.
+
+### F8 — Cost and audit ledger
+
+Every model call appends tokens and cost to the classification record. A
+`GET /metrics` response and a `scripts/report_costs.py` script produce
+cost-per-event and total figures for the executive readout. Prompt text is
+versioned (`prompt_version`) so results are reproducible.
+
+### F9 — Run script and evidence
+
+`scripts/run_pipeline.py` ingests, classifies, and prints a table of
+event id, bucket, confidence and one-line rationale, and writes
+`evidence/run-<timestamp>.json` containing the full results, so the
+checkpoint can be verified by someone who did not build it.
+
+## Quality Bar
+
+- `pytest` passes; tests for F2, F4, F5, F6 do not require network or DIAL
+  (use a fake client). One opt-in integration test hits DIAL for real when
+  `DIAL_API_KEY` is set.
+- Acceptance criteria in every story are observable behaviours (an endpoint
+  response, a persisted record, a test that passes), not intentions.
+- No secrets in code, tests, or docs.
+
+## Out of Scope (for this slice — follow-on paid work per the discovery call)
+
+- Live source connectors or web scraping; the real source list is not yet
+  provided.
+- Applicability/impact assessment, action recommendation, communication
+  drafting, knowledge learning, dashboards and reporting (RFP capabilities
+  3–7).
+- Integrations: SharePoint, Entra ID, Azure AI Search, Littler, MRL,
+  PeopleSoft, GovDocs, Equifax, LMS, Teams/Outlook.
+- Authentication and multi-user access control on the review portal.
+- Topics beyond Wage & Hour.
+
+## Open Questions — keep open; do not resolve inside stories
+
+Stories may reference these as assumptions but must not invent answers.
+
+1. Real source list, keywords, and topic instructions — not yet provided by
+   Darden (transcript item 3).
+2. Darden's SOPs and decision criteria for relevant vs. not — not provided;
+   the fixture tags are not an answer key.
+3. The current Excel tracker format — not provided; no import is built.
+4. MVP success/exit criteria — undefined; the proposal must propose
+   measurable ones (transcript item 4). A working assumption for the
+   prototype: agreement with the fixture's design intent on 11/11 items with
+   every ambiguous item landing in `needs-review`.
+5. Confidence threshold and who owns it — SME control per the RFP; default
+   0.70 is a placeholder.
+6. Whether an existing EPAM accelerator can replace parts of this build
+   (transcript item 7).
+7. Staffing lead time for Foundry/Azure skills (transcript item 6).
+8. Production gateway is Azure AI Foundry; DIAL is the build-time stand-in
+   only.
