+# Secure engineering practices for this exercise
+
+- `.env` holds a real DIAL API key. Never read, print, log, or paste its
+  contents — not into chat, not into a commit, not into a comment. Load it
+  only via `dial/client.py` (which uses `python-dotenv`).
+- If a participant pastes a key directly into a prompt by mistake, do not
+  echo it back or write it into any file — tell them to rotate the key and
+  put it in `.env` instead.
+- Never hardcode `DIAL_API_KEY`, `DIAL_API_ENDPOINT`, or any secret value in
+  application code, tests, or docs — read from environment only.
+- `.env` is gitignored. If you ever see it staged for commit, stop and flag
+  it instead of committing.
+- `.env.example` has no real values — it's safe to read, edit, and commit.
+- This is enforced mechanically, not just by convention — see
+  `.claude/hooks/scan-prompt.py` (blocks secrets in prompts) and
+  `.claude/hooks/guard-env-files.py` (blocks reading `.env` by any route).
+  Don't remove or weaken either.
